## [x] T22 · Make missing tree-sitter grammars visible; fix the grammar test  [ASK]

Files: src/dk-treesit.el (~99-103), test/dk-integration-test.el (~28-43)
Problem: no grammars are installed (~/.config/emacs/tree-sitter missing), so
.go/go.mod/YAML/CMake/Dockerfile open in text-mode (no gopls, no gofmt), C
and Rust use classic modes, and the only signal is a *Messages* line.  The
integration test then fails for environmental reasons.
Question: "Should I also install the pinned grammars now (network + C
compiler, writes ~/.config/emacs/tree-sitter, git-ignored)?"
  1. (Recommended) Yes, run dk/treesit-install-all in batch after the code change.
  2. No, only the code/test change.
Decision: option 1 — install grammars now.
Change:
  - Replace the startup `message` with `(display-warning 'dk-treesit
    (format ...) :warning)` so it lands in *Warnings*.
  - In `dk/installed-grammars-select-working-modes`, `skip-unless` all
    grammars needed by the cases are available, so the test reports
    "skipped" instead of failing on machines without grammars.
  - If option 1: run in the REAL init dir (this is the one sanctioned
    exception, it only writes the git-ignored tree-sitter/ dir):
      emacs --batch --init-directory="$HOME/.config/emacs" \
        --load early-init.el --eval '(package-activate-all)' \
        --load init.el --eval '(dk/treesit-install-all)'
Verify: smoke test shows the warning when grammars are absent; ERT shows the
grammar test skipped (absent) or passed (installed).
Commit: "Warn about missing tree-sitter grammars"
Result: src/dk-treesit.el (display-warning), test/dk-integration-test.el (skip-unless).  Without
grammars: warning shown, test skipped.  Installed all 12 grammars in ~/.config/emacs/tree-sitter;
with them: no warning, 6/6 ERT pass.  Restart the daemon to pick up ts modes.


## [x] T23 · Ask before recursive deletes on remote dired

Files: src/dk-functions.el, src/dk-navigation.el (~25-41)
Problem: `dired-recursive-deletes 'always` relies on the trash, but over
TRAMP there is no trash: D permanently deletes directory trees after one
prompt.
Change: add `dk/dired-remote-safety` (dk-functions.el): when
`(file-remote-p default-directory)`, `(setq-local dired-recursive-deletes
'top)`.  Hook it on `dired-mode-hook` from the dired `use-package`.
Comment: locally the trash makes 'always safe; remotely it is permanent.
(Optional, only if the user dired's into BSD/macOS hosts: note in the
comment that `--group-directories-first` needs GNU ls remotely.)
Verify: in a dired-mode temp buffer with a remote-looking
`default-directory` stubbed via cl-letf on `file-remote-p`, the hook sets
the local value to `top`; a local dired buffer keeps `always`.
Commit: "Confirm recursive deletes in remote dired"
Result: src/dk-functions.el (`dk/dired-remote-safety'), src/dk-navigation.el (hook, comments incl.
GNU ls note), cheatsheet.md.  Stubbed remote dired -> top; local dired -> always (not local).


## [x] T24 · Sane kill-ring/search-ring persistence  [ASK]

Files: src/dk-defaults.el (~46-56), src/dk-functions.el
Problem: the kill-ring is saved in plain text to `history` (anything
copied, passwords included) with font-lock text properties (file bloat),
and the savehist caps (200) exceed the ring sizes (kill-ring-max 120,
search-ring-max 16), so the numbers mean nothing.
Question: "Keep persisting the kill-ring across restarts?"
  1. (Recommended) Yes, but strip text properties and keep caps consistent.
  2. No, persist only search rings.
Decision: 1 — yes, persist the kill-ring; strip text properties, consistent caps.
Change:
  - Set the ring sizes explicitly once (e.g. kill-ring-max 200,
    search-ring-max 100, regexp-search-ring-max 100) and register the
    variables in `savehist-additional-variables` without separate caps.
  - Option 1: add `dk/savehist-strip-kill-ring` to `savehist-save-hook`:
    replace kill-ring with `substring-no-properties` copies of its string
    entries.  Docstring notes the trade-off (live entries lose properties
    such as yank handlers at each save).
  - Option 2: drop kill-ring from the list.
Verify: variable values; run `dk/savehist-strip-kill-ring` on a kill-ring
containing a propertized string -> entries have no properties.
Commit: "Keep savehist ring limits consistent"
Result: src/dk-defaults.el (ring sizes 200/100/100, plain variable list, hook),
src/dk-functions.el (`dk/savehist-strip-kill-ring', in place so the yank pointer
stays valid), cheatsheet.md.  Verified values; propertized entries stripped.


## [x] T25 · Move lock files out of project trees; fix .gitignore gaps

Files: src/dk-vars.el, src/dk-defaults.el (~13), .gitignore
Problem: `create-lockfiles t` writes .#file symlinks next to edited files
(git status noise, file-watcher triggers).  Also `autosave/` (used by
`dk/auto-save-directory`) and `*.elc` (made by `dk/recompile-config`) are
not git-ignored.
Change:
  - `dk/lock-directory` in dk-vars.el ("locks/" under user-emacs-directory,
    trailing slash, docstring like the auto-save one).
  - In dk-defaults.el: `make-directory` it and set
    `lock-file-name-transforms` to `((".*" ,dk/lock-directory t))`.
    Comment: locking still protects against other local Emacs sessions; for
    TRAMP files cross-machine lock detection is lost (acceptable).
  - .gitignore: add `autosave/`, `locks/`, `*.elc`.
Verify: in the sandbox, visit a temp file, modify it, check that the lock
appears under $SB/locks/ and not beside the file.
Commit: "Keep lock files out of project directories"
Result: src/dk-vars.el (`dk/lock-directory'), src/dk-defaults.el
(`lock-file-name-transforms'), .gitignore (autosave/, locks/, *.elc).  Sandbox:
lock created under $SB/locks/, none beside the file.


